## TL;DR  
**No – the `font-faces` block that works in MapLibre Native cannot be dropped straight into a MapLibre GL JS style.**  

GL JS does not read local `font-faces` definitions; it only draws text from *glyph-pbf* files that are fetched from a URL given in the top-level `glyphs` property of the style. If you need offline fonts in a web app you have to provide those glyph files yourself (or run a tiny glyph-server) and point the style at them.  

Below you’ll find:

1. **Why the native `font-faces` block doesn’t apply to GL JS** – a quick look at the spec differences.  
2. **How to get the same “offline-font” experience in GL JS** – three practical approaches (self-hosted glyph PBFs, a tiny Node/Express glyph server, or embedding a pre-bundled glyph sprite).  
3. **Step-by-step guide to generate the glyph PBFs from the same TTF/OTF files you already use** (including Unicode-range handling).  
4. **Sample style JSON and minimal HTML/JS to wire it up**.  
5. **Gotchas & performance tips**.  

---

## 1. Why `font-faces` is a *native-only* feature

| Feature | MapLibre Native | MapLibre GL JS |
|---------|----------------|----------------|
| `font-faces` block in the style JSON | **Supported** – reads local `.ttf/.otf` files, can specify `unicode-range` per file, works completely offline. | **Not supported** – the GL JS style spec (v8) does **not** contain a `font-faces` member. GL JS never touches the file system; it only knows about glyphs that are delivered as **PBF** tiles from a *glyph server*. |
| Text rendering pipeline | Uses FreeType (or platform-specific font rasterizer) to turn the supplied fonts into glyph bitmaps on-the-fly. | Uses **Mapbox GL’s glyph-pbf** format (a compact binary representation of vector outlines). The browser fetches those PBFs via XHR/Fetch. |
| Offline capability | Achieved by bundling the font files and the `font-faces` block. | Achieved by bundling **glyph-pbf files** and serving them from a URL that works without an internet connection (e.g., `./fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf`). |

Because GL JS never reads a `font-faces` array, copying that block into a GL JS style will be ignored (or cause a validation error) and the map will fall back to the default glyph URL (`https://fonts.openmaptiles.org/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf`), which obviously requires network access.

---

## 2. How to get “offline fonts” in GL JS

There are three common ways to make GL JS work without an external glyph service:

| Approach | What you ship | How you reference it in the style | Typical effort |
|----------|---------------|-----------------------------------|----------------|
| **Self-hosted glyph PBFs** (static files) | A folder tree like `fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf` generated from your TTF/OTF files. | Set `"glyphs": "./fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf"` (relative to the HTML page or absolute URL). | Medium – you need a build step to generate the PBFs, but no server code. |
| **Tiny Node/Express glyph server** | Same PBF files, but served via a small HTTP endpoint (`/fonts/:fontstack/:range.pbf`). | Same `"glyphs"` URL, just point to `http://localhost:3000/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf`. | Low – just run a one-liner server; useful for dev or when you already have a backend. |
| **Bundle glyphs into a single data-uri** (experimental) | Encode each PBF as a base-64 data URI and list them in a custom `glyphs` object (requires a custom loader). | Not part of the official spec; you’d need to monkey-patch GL JS’s `GlyphManager`. | High – only for demos or very small font sets. |

**The most straightforward for a production web app is the first option: static glyph files served from the same origin as your HTML/JS.**  
That way the whole app can be cached by a Service Worker or packaged as a PWA and truly works offline.

---

## 3. Generating the glyph-PBF files from your existing fonts

You already have the TTF/OTF files and the Unicode-range split you used in the native `font-faces`. The same split can be used when generating glyph PBFs, which reduces file size and keeps the same “font-per-range” logic.

### 3.1 Tools you can use

| Tool | Install | Command (example) |
|------|---------|-------------------|
| **`glyphhanger` + `fontnik`** (Mapbox’s pipeline) | `npm i -g glyphhanger fontnik` | See script below. |
| **`mapbox-gl-native`’s `generate-glyph-pbf`** (C++ binary) | Build from source – heavy. | Not recommended for most web devs. |
| **`tippecanoe` + `ttf2pbf`** (community) | `npm i -g ttf2pbf` | Simpler but less configurable. |
| **`mapbox-gl-glyph-pbf` (Node library)** | `npm i mapbox-gl-glyph-pbf` | Programmatic generation. |

Below is a **complete Node script** that:

1. Reads a list of font files + Unicode ranges (mirroring your native `font-faces`).  
2. Uses `glyphhanger` to extract the exact glyphs needed for each range.  
3. Feeds those glyphs to `fontnik` to produce the PBF files.  
4. Writes them to `./fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf`.

```js
// generate-glyphs.js
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// Prereqs: npm i -g glyphhanger fontnik
// ------------------------------------------------------------
const { execSync } = require('child_process');
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Define your font-faces exactly as you have in the native style
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
const fontFaces = [
  {
    family: 'MySans',
    src: './fonts/MySans-Regular.ttf',
    unicodeRange: 'U+0000-00FF', // Basic Latin + Latin-1 Supplement
  },
  {
    family: 'MySans',
    src: './fonts/MySans-Regular-CJK.ttf',
    unicodeRange: 'U+4E00-9FFF', // CJK Unified Ideographs
  },
  // add more entries as needed …
];

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Helper to turn a Unicode range string into a glyph list
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
function rangeToGlyphs(range) {
  // glyphhanger expects a CSS-style range, e.g. "U+0000-00FF"
  // It will output a list of code points like "0x20,0x21,…"
  const out = execSync(`glyphhanger --unicode-range="${range}" --format=hex`, {
    encoding: 'utf8',
  });
  // glyphhanger prints a space-separated list; we need a comma-separated list
  return out.trim().replace(/\s+/g, ',');
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Generate the PBF for each font-face
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
fontFaces.forEach(face => {
  const glyphs = rangeToGlyphs(face.unicodeRange);
  const outDir = path.join('fonts', face.family);
  if (!fs.existsSync(outDir)) fs.mkdirSync(outDir, { recursive: true });

  // Fontnik command:
  //   fontnik <ttf> <output.pbf> --glyphs <comma-list>
  const outPath = path.join(outDir, `${face.unicodeRange.replace(/[^\w]/g, '_')}.pbf`);
  const cmd = `fontnik "${face.src}" "${outPath}" --glyphs ${glyphs}`;
  console.log(`Generating ${outPath} …`);
  execSync(cmd, { stdio: 'inherit' });
});

console.log('✅ All glyph PBFs generated.');
```

**How to run it**

```bash
node generate-glyphs.js
```

Resulting folder layout:

```
public/
 └─ fonts/
     └─ MySans/
         ├─ U_0000_00FF.pbf
         └─ U_4E00_9FFF.pbf
```

> **Note** – The file name can be anything; GL JS only cares that the URL pattern matches `{fontstack}/{range}.pbf`. The convention `U_XXXX_YYYY.pbf` is just convenient.

### 3.2 Verifying the glyphs

You can quickly test a generated PBF with the `fontnik` CLI:

```bash
fontnik ./fonts/MySans-Regular.ttf ./tmp.pbf --glyphs 0x41,0x42,0x43
# then open ./tmp.pbf in a hex editor or use a small script to decode it.
```

If you see a non-zero file size and no errors, you’re good to go.

---

## 4. Wire the glyphs into your GL JS style

### 4.1 Minimal style JSON

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "osm": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "mbtiles://{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"
    }
  },
  "glyphs": "./fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "water-label",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "water_name",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": ["MySans Regular"],   // <-- matches the family name you used
        "text-size": 12
      },
      "paint": {
        "text-color": "#2c3e50"
      }
    }
    // … other layers …
  ]
}
```

**Key points**

* `glyphs` uses the **same placeholder syntax** (`{fontstack}` and `{range}`) that GL JS expects.  
* `text-font` must reference the **font family name** exactly as it appears in the TTF’s `name` table (e.g., `"MySans Regular"`).  
* The Unicode range is **automatically selected** by GL JS: when it needs a glyph for code point `U+4E01`, it will request `./fonts/MySans/U_4E00_9FFF.pbf`. If that file does not exist, it falls back to the next range (or the default glyph server).  

### 4.2 Minimal HTML/JS

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Offline MapLibre GL JS with Local Fonts</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1" />
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import { Map } from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.esm.js';

  const map = new Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: './style.json',          // the JSON from §4.1
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    // If you want a true offline experience, also set:
    //   preserveDrawingBuffer: true,
    //   fadeDuration: 0,
  });

  // Optional: register a Service Worker to cache the glyph PBFs
  if ('serviceWorker' in navigator) {
    navigator.serviceWorker.register('sw.js');
  }
</script>
</body>
